{
  id:'npqb-nr-119', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Indication for neuroimaging in headache:',
  options:['Headache with vomiting','Headache with plantar extensor','Severe headache','Associated with colored illusions'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**An extensor plantar response is an objective upper-motor-neurone sign, and a headache accompanied by one is a structural headache until imaging proves otherwise.** That is what makes option b the indication — not the severity of the pain, but the presence of a hard neurological finding beside it.\n\nThe cached decks establish the sign itself even though no headache lecture exists. `L1) INTRODUCTION AND LOCALISATION IN NEUROLOGY` lists the clinical picture of a pyramidal lesion as *"Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes / Absent superficial reflexes / Extensor plantar"*, and `L12) hemi,parap&ataxia` repeats it for the established phase of hemiplegia — *"Reflexes are exaggerated, plantar extensor"*. An extensor plantar is therefore never a feature of a primary headache: migraine, tension-type and cluster headache are all normal on examination between and during attacks. Its appearance means the corticospinal tract is involved, which points to a tumour, an abscess, a haemorrhage, an infarct or demyelination — every one of which is a neuroimaging diagnosis.\n\nThe general framework the question is drawn from is the red-flag list often taught as **SNOOP** *(not taken from the course material)*: **S**ystemic symptoms or secondary risk factors (fever, weight loss, cancer, immunosuppression), **N**eurological signs or symptoms, **O**nset that is sudden or thunderclap, **O**lder age at first onset (new headache over 50), and **P**attern change or positional/progressive features. Option b is the "N" of that list in its purest form.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not by itself mandate imaging |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Headache with vomiting** | Vomiting is a *defining associated feature of migraine*, present in a large share of ordinary attacks. It only becomes a red flag when it is early-morning, projectile, and joined by papilledema — the raised-intracranial-pressure pattern. Alone it is unremarkable |\n| **c. Severe headache** | Severity alone is the weakest of all red flags. Cluster headache is among the most severe pains in medicine and is entirely benign structurally; migraine is routinely described as severe. What matters is *sudden* onset (thunderclap), not intensity |\n| **d. Associated with colored illusions** | This describes *visual aura* — the luminous, coloured, zigzag phenomena that precede classic migraine. It is a reassuring, expected feature of migraine with aura, not an indication to image. Only an aura that is always on the same side, lasts over an hour, or first appears in later life raises concern |\n\nThe trap the examiner is setting is that three of the four options are things patients complain about loudly, and the correct one is something the *doctor* finds on examination. Headache assessment is not decided by how the pain is described; it is decided by whether the neurological examination is normal. `L8) Cranial nerves` makes the same point on the other classic red-flag sign, instructing the student to *"check for swelling of the optic disc (papilledema), which can indicate increased intracranial pressure"*.\n\n**Bottom line:** image the headache patient who has an abnormal neurological examination, a thunderclap onset, a first headache after 50, or a definite change in pattern. Do not image for severity, and do not image for nausea and aura, which are the ordinary furniture of migraine.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A headache with any objective neurological sign — an extensor plantar above all — requires neuroimaging; severity, vomiting and visual aura alone do not.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30). The bank prints option b as "Headache with planter extensor"; the obvious typo for *plantar* is corrected here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-120', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A 20 yrs. old female presented with attacks of headache; unilateral or bilateral, pulsating in character, some attacks are associated with nausea and photophobia. What drugs can be used to **prevent** the attacks?',
  options:['Nifedipine and Sumatriptan','Sumatriptan and Amitriptyline','Paracetamol and Nifedipine','Propranolol and Amitriptyline','Sodium valproate and sumatriptan'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The vignette is migraine, and the word the whole question turns on is *prevent*: only option d pairs two genuine prophylactic agents, propranolol and amitriptyline.** Every other option smuggles an *abortive* drug into a prophylaxis question.\n\nThe clinical picture is textbook migraine — a young woman, recurrent attacks, pulsating quality, unilateral or bilateral, with nausea and photophobia. `L1) INTRODUCTION AND LOCALISATION IN NEUROLOGY` recognises migraine as a neurological entity in its own right, listing *"Migraine syndrome"* among the causes of hemiplegia and characterising its tempo as *"Migraine - progression over seconds to minutes"*, which separates it from the sudden onset of vascular disease and the slower march of a space-occupying lesion.\n\n**The abortive/preventive split is the single most examined idea in headache pharmacology** *(the detail below is not taken from the course material — no headache deck exists in the supplied lectures)*:\n\n| | Purpose | Taken | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Abortive (acute)** | Stop an attack that has begun | At onset of an attack | Simple analgesics, NSAIDs, paracetamol + metoclopramide, **triptans** (sumatriptan), ergotamine |\n| **Preventive (prophylactic)** | Reduce attack frequency and severity | Daily, regardless of attacks | **Propranolol** (beta-blocker), **amitriptyline** (TCA), sodium valproate, topiramate, flunarizine, candesartan |\n\n**Propranolol** is the classic first-line migraine prophylactic — a non-selective beta-blocker, and the one most syllabi name first. **Amitriptyline** is a tricyclic antidepressant used at doses far below the antidepressant range; the same drug appears in `L8) Cranial nerves` for facial pain, where the deck lists *"Antidepressants: Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline"* among the treatments of trigeminal neuralgia. Its usefulness in headache is independent of any effect on mood, which is a favourite examiner point.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Nifedipine and Sumatriptan** — sumatriptan is purely abortive, and nifedipine is a dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker whose vasodilatation typically *causes* headache rather than preventing it. The calcium-channel blocker with a migraine-prophylaxis reputation is flunarizine, and verapamil belongs to cluster headache.\n- **b. Sumatriptan and Amitriptyline** — half right. Amitriptyline is a proper prophylactic; sumatriptan is not, and taking a triptan daily produces medication-overuse headache, which makes the problem worse.\n- **c. Paracetamol and Nifedipine** — paracetamol is an acute analgesic and nifedipine is not a prophylactic; neither belongs.\n- **e. Sodium valproate and sumatriptan** — again half right, and the same error as b. **Sodium valproate genuinely is a migraine prophylactic**, so a student who reads only the first drug will pick this; sumatriptan disqualifies it. This is the best distractor on the page and rewards reading the whole option as a sentence.\n\n**Bottom line:** in a migraine question, first decide whether you are being asked to *abort* or to *prevent*, then reject any option containing a triptan or ergot if the answer is prevention. Propranolol and amitriptyline are the two names to have ready.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Migraine prophylaxis is propranolol, amitriptyline, valproate or topiramate taken daily; triptans and ergots are abortive only and never prophylactic.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30). The bank spells options b and d "Amitryptilin" and option e "Na valproate and sumitriptan"; spellings normalised here.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-121', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A change in headache pattern, such as increasing pain, frequency, or the development of new features, may indicate:',
  options:['Migraine progression','A need for stronger pain medications','A secondary cause, such as a tumor or increased intracranial pressure','Improved response to current treatment'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**A headache that changes its established pattern is a red flag for a secondary cause, and that is what option c states.** This is the "P" of the SNOOP red-flag mnemonic — pattern change — and it is the reason clinicians ask every chronic headache patient whether this headache is *the same as usual*.\n\nThe logic is that primary headaches are, by definition, stereotyped. A migraineur has attacks that resemble each other for years; a tension-type sufferer has the same band-like ache. When the character, frequency or severity shifts, or when a new feature appears that was never part of the syndrome — vomiting, a focal deficit, a seizure, personality change, a headache that is now worse in the morning or on coughing — the assumption that the diagnosis is still the old primary headache no longer holds. Something new has been added, and the commonest somethings are a space-occupying lesion and raised intracranial pressure.\n\nThe supplied decks support the raised-pressure half of the answer directly. `L8) Cranial nerves` teaches fundoscopy precisely to *"check for swelling of the optic disc (papilledema), which can indicate increased intracranial pressure"*, and later poses its own question about a patient *"presenting to you with papilledema, headache and significant obstructive hydrocephalus"* — the classic triad in which headache is only one component. `L10) Stroke` likewise lists *"Increased intracranial pressure with vomiting"* among the features that mark a headache as structural rather than benign.\n\nThe classic raised-ICP headache profile *(not taken from the course material)*: worse in the early morning or on waking, worsened by coughing, straining or bending forward, accompanied by vomiting that may be projectile and may relieve the pain, and eventually joined by papilledema, a sixth-nerve palsy as a false localising sign, and a falling level of consciousness.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Migraine progression** — this is the seductive answer, because migraine frequency genuinely does fluctuate and can transform into chronic migraine. But "migraine progression" is a *diagnosis of exclusion made after imaging is clear*, not the thing a pattern change indicates. Assuming it is the safest-sounding option and the most dangerous one: it is exactly the reasoning that lets a tumour be treated as a bad month of migraines.\n- **b. A need for stronger pain medications** — this inverts the clinical priority, treating a diagnostic alarm as an analgesic dosing problem. It is also the pathway to medication-overuse headache, in which escalating analgesia is itself the cause of the worsening pattern.\n- **d. Improved response to current treatment** — self-contradictory. Increasing pain and frequency are the definition of a *failing* response.\n\n**Bottom line:** "the same headache, only worse" is a phrase that should prompt examination and imaging, not a stronger prescription. The two questions that matter at every headache follow-up are *is this the same headache you have always had?* and *is your neurological examination still normal?*\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A change in an established headache pattern — more pain, more attacks, or new features — signals a secondary cause such as tumour or raised intracranial pressure and demands reassessment, not stronger analgesia.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-122', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A disheveled male, seems to be in the 3rd decade of his life, came to you in the E.R. at 3:00 A.M. He was screaming frightenedly "please help me; I have a very severe headache as if a thunderclap is striking my head. this denotes that my enemies are going to kill me as soon as possible. please help me". The best management to be done for him is:',
  options:['Giving him 3 cc saline intravenously and then telling him "you will be fine. Go home".','Advising him to leave the E.R. and go to a psychiatrist.','Prescribing antipsychotic drug for him and tell him to follow up with a psychiatrist.','Verification of the nature of his headache and asking for a psychiatric consultation.'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The patient has two problems at once — a thunderclap headache and a persecutory delusion — and option d is the only one that addresses both.** The examiner is testing whether a psychiatric presentation will make you abandon a medical emergency.\n\n`L10) Stroke` defines the term the patient himself uses, and it is a neurosurgical emergency. Subarachnoid haemorrhage presents with *"Thunderclap headache with two important criteria: very severe headache described as the worst headache of my life"* and *"Thunderclap, which means sudden onset of severe headache, in contrast to migrainous hedache, which begins with mild headache then progresses to moderate or severe headache within 30-60 minutes."* The deck adds that *"In 85% of the cases, ruptured aneurysm"* is the cause, that *"A significant proportion of patients die immediately before reaching the hospital"*, and that investigation is *"CT and/or MRI brain"* followed by *"Vascular imaging using CTA/MRA as well as diagnostic cerebral angiography (DSA): to demonstrate the aneurysm."* Rebleeding *"can be fatal, so that urgent treatment of the aneurysm is recommended"*.\n\nSo "verification of the nature of his headache" is not a euphemism for reassurance — it means taking the history properly and imaging the head. The second clause, "asking for a psychiatric consultation", is equally necessary: *"my enemies are going to kill me"* is a persecutory delusion, and the dishevelled appearance and 3 a.m. presentation support an active psychiatric illness. Neither problem cancels the other.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. 3 cc saline intravenously and "you will be fine. Go home"** — this is a deliberate placebo plus discharge. It is wrong three times over: it fails to investigate a possible subarachnoid haemorrhage, it is a deception that breaches informed consent, and it discharges an acutely psychotic patient into the night. Giving a placebo to a distressed patient to prove the complaint is not real is indefensible.\n- **b. Advising him to leave the E.R. and go to a psychiatrist** — this diagnoses the psychiatric problem correctly and then abandons the neurological one, sending a possible aneurysmal bleed out of the one building with a CT scanner. It also makes the referral the patient\'s own responsibility at the moment he is least able to carry it.\n- **c. Prescribing an antipsychotic and telling him to follow up** — treats the delusion, ignores the headache, and starts an antipsychotic without assessment in a patient whose agitation may itself be a symptom of an intracranial event. Sedating a patient with a possible subarachnoid haemorrhage also removes the conscious level, the single most important thing to monitor.\n\n**The general principle** *(not taken from the course material)*: a psychiatric history does not confer immunity from physical illness, and patients with severe mental illness have *worse* physical outcomes partly because their somatic complaints are discounted. This phenomenon has a name — diagnostic overshadowing — and this question is a clean illustration of it.\n\n**Bottom line:** investigate the headache and get psychiatry involved. Do the medical workup first, in parallel with, never instead of, the psychiatric assessment.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A thunderclap headache is a subarachnoid haemorrhage until imaging says otherwise, even when the patient is psychotic — investigate the headache and obtain psychiatric assessment in parallel, never one instead of the other.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30). The bank prints "striking me head", "E.R on 3.00A.M." and "the e.r"; punctuation normalised, wording preserved.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-123', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A patient presents with pain that is boring in character in the periorbital area, which awakens them daily from sleep at 2 AM, accompanied by ptosis and lacrimation. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Tension headache','Cluster headache','Migraine','Sinusitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Every element of this vignette is a diagnostic criterion of cluster headache: strictly periorbital site, boring quality, nocturnal clockwork timing, and ipsilateral cranial autonomic features.** No other primary headache assembles that combination.\n\nCluster headache is the prototype of the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias *(the classification detail is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*. Its defining features:\n\n| Feature | Cluster headache |\n|---|---|\n| **Site** | Strictly unilateral, orbital, supraorbital or temporal — it does not swap sides within a bout |\n| **Quality** | Boring, drilling, "like a hot poker in the eye" — among the most severe pains described in medicine |\n| **Duration** | 15-180 minutes untreated |\n| **Periodicity** | Clockwork: same hour each day, classically waking the patient 1-2 hours after falling asleep, in bouts lasting weeks with long remissions |\n| **Autonomic features** | Ipsilateral **ptosis** and miosis (a partial Horner syndrome), **lacrimation**, conjunctival injection, rhinorrhoea or nasal blockage, eyelid oedema, facial sweating |\n| **Behaviour in attack** | Restless and agitated — the patient paces, rocks, cannot lie still |\n| **Demographics** | Male predominance, onset typically third decade; alcohol triggers an attack during a bout |\n\nThe ptosis and lacrimation are what settle this question. They are parasympathetic and sympathetic phenomena driven from the same trigeminal-autonomic reflex, and they are ipsilateral to the pain. Note that the incomplete Horner syndrome of cluster headache — ptosis and miosis without anhidrosis — can persist between attacks.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Tension headache** — bilateral, band-like, pressing or tightening rather than boring, mild to moderate, with **no autonomic features at all** and no nocturnal periodicity. Tension-type headache characteristically does not wake the patient from sleep.\n- **c. Migraine** — the closest competitor and the reason the question is worth asking. Migraine is also unilateral and severe, but its quality is **pulsating** not boring, its duration is 4-72 hours not 15-180 minutes, its associated features are **nausea, vomiting, photophobia and phonophobia** rather than lacrimation and ptosis, and the migraineur **lies still in a dark room** whereas the cluster patient paces. Migraine has no clockwork nocturnal alarm.\n- **d. Sinusitis** — produces facial pain and pressure that is typically worse on bending forward, accompanied by purulent nasal discharge, fever and tenderness over the affected sinus, and it does not produce ptosis. Sinus disease is a common misdiagnosis in both cluster headache and migraine, precisely because rhinorrhoea and nasal blockage are genuine cluster autonomic features — which is a trap in the opposite direction.\n\n**Treatment, since it is examined alongside the diagnosis:** acute attacks are aborted with **high-flow 100% oxygen** and **subcutaneous sumatriptan**; **verapamil** is the prophylactic of choice, which is the subject of `npqb-nr-131` on the same page.\n\n**Bottom line:** unilateral periorbital boring pain + autonomic features + clockwork nocturnal timing = cluster headache. The autonomic signs are what separate it from migraine.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Cluster headache is strictly unilateral boring periorbital pain in short nocturnal clockwork attacks with ipsilateral ptosis and lacrimation; autonomic features and a restless patient distinguish it from migraine.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-124', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'The drug used in **aborting** a migraine attack is:',
  options:['Levodopa','Sumatriptan','Propranolol','Topiramate'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Sumatriptan is the abortive drug; the other three are either prophylactics or belong to a different disease entirely.** This is the mirror image of `npqb-nr-120` on the previous page, which asked the same distinction the other way round, and the two together are the clearest sign that this bank considers the abortive/preventive split the core of migraine pharmacology.\n\n**Sumatriptan** is a serotonin **5-HT1B/1D receptor agonist** — the mechanism the bank asks directly in `npqb-nr-130`, where the key is "serotonergic agonists". Its two actions map onto the two components of a migraine attack: agonism at 5-HT1B on cranial vessels produces vasoconstriction of the dilated meningeal arteries, and agonism at 5-HT1D on trigeminal nerve terminals inhibits the release of the vasoactive neuropeptides (CGRP, substance P) that drive neurogenic inflammation. It is taken **at the onset of an attack**, works best when taken early, and is available orally, intranasally and subcutaneously — the subcutaneous route being the fastest and the one used in cluster headache.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is | Where it belongs |\n|---|---|---|\n| **a. Levodopa** | Dopamine precursor | **Parkinson disease.** `L3) MOVEMENT DISORDERS` is the deck that carries it. It has no role in headache at all, and is the only option here that is not a headache drug in any capacity |\n| **c. Propranolol** | Non-selective beta-blocker | **Migraine prophylaxis** — first-line, taken daily. Correct drug, wrong question: it does nothing for an attack already under way |\n| **d. Topiramate** | Antiepileptic (carbonic anhydrase inhibition, sodium-channel blockade, GABA potentiation) | **Migraine prophylaxis** — and the answer to `npqb-nr-127` on the next page, which asks which antiepileptic prevents migraine |\n\nOptions c and d are therefore not random distractors: they are both genuine migraine drugs, and both are keyed correct in *other questions in this same topic*. What disqualifies them here is the single word **aborting**. A student who has memorised a list of "migraine drugs" without sorting it by purpose has a one-in-three chance on this question; a student who has sorted it answers instantly.\n\n**Practical sequence for an acute migraine attack** *(not taken from the course material — no headache deck exists in the supplied lectures)*: simple analgesic or paracetamol with an antiemetic first, NSAIDs second, and a **triptan** when those fail — which is exactly the ladder the bank prints in its own explanation box for `npqb-nr-135`. Ergotamine is the older abortive, largely displaced by triptans because of its poorer side-effect and vasoconstriction profile.\n\n**Two cautions worth carrying:** triptans are **contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension and previous stroke** because they are vasoconstrictors — the reason `npqb-nr-126` keys ketorolac rather than sumatriptan for a cardiac patient. And triptans taken on more than about 10 days a month cause **medication-overuse headache**.\n\n**Bottom line:** sumatriptan aborts, propranolol and topiramate prevent, levodopa is not a headache drug.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sumatriptan, a 5-HT1B/1D agonist, is the abortive migraine drug; propranolol and topiramate are prophylactics and levodopa has no headache role.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-125', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Migraine is best classified as which of the following?',
  options:['VC cause psychiatric manifestations','Nociceptive','Neuropathy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Migraine pain is nociceptive — it arises from activation of pain receptors on intracranial structures, not from damage to a nerve.** The bank keys b, and the classification being tested is the standard division of pain into nociceptive and neuropathic.\n\n**Nociceptive pain** is generated when nociceptors in a structurally intact nervous system are stimulated by tissue injury, inflammation or mechanical distortion. **Neuropathic pain** is generated by a lesion or disease *of the somatosensory nervous system itself*, and is described as burning, electric, shooting or shock-like, often with allodynia.\n\nMigraine sits on the nociceptive side. The brain parenchyma has no pain receptors; the pain-sensitive intracranial structures are the meninges, the large cerebral and meningeal vessels, and the venous sinuses, all innervated by the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. In migraine, activation of this **trigeminovascular system** releases vasoactive neuropeptides — CGRP and substance P — producing neurogenic inflammation and sensitisation of perivascular trigeminal afferents. The receptors are being stimulated; the nerve is not diseased. Central sensitisation during a long attack explains cutaneous allodynia, but the primary process remains nociceptor activation *(this mechanistic account is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture)*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **c. Neuropathy** is the option worth pausing over, because the supplied material describes a facial pain that genuinely *is* neuropathic, and it is not migraine. `L8) Cranial nerves` teaches trigeminal neuralgia as pain that is *"often described as electric, shock-like or stabbing"*, lasting *"several seconds"*, arising from *"Compression by an aberrant loop of an artery or vein"* — a lesion of the nerve itself. Its treatment is correspondingly neuropathic: the deck lists *"Carbamazepine, Oxcarbazepine, Gabapentin, Pregabalin"* and *"Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline"*. That migraine responds to triptans and NSAIDs while trigeminal neuralgia responds to antiepileptics is the pharmacological fingerprint of the nociceptive/neuropathic distinction.\n- **a. "VC cause psychiatric manifestations"** is garbled in the source and is not a pain classification at all. Read as written it appears to assert that migraine causes psychiatric manifestations. Migraine is genuinely comorbid with depression and anxiety, but comorbidity is not a *classification* of the pain, and the option does not answer the question asked.\n\n**Note on the option list:** this question prints only **three** options (a, b, c) — confirmed at 200 dpi, so it is not a rendering artefact. Three-option questions occur elsewhere in this bank.\n\n**A second useful classification** the question gestures at: migraine is a **primary** headache — the pain is the disease — as opposed to a **secondary** headache, where the pain is a symptom of another disorder (tumour, haemorrhage, meningitis, giant cell arteritis). Both classifications are examinable, and the bank tests the primary/secondary axis separately in `npqb-nr-139`.\n\n**Bottom line:** migraine is nociceptive and primary; trigeminal neuralgia is the neuropathic facial pain, and their drug classes differ accordingly.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Migraine is nociceptive pain from trigeminovascular activation of pain-sensitive meninges and vessels; trigeminal neuralgia is the neuropathic facial pain, which is why one responds to triptans and the other to antiepileptics.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.27 (answer p.30). ⚠️ Prints only three options; option a is garbled in the source and is transcribed as printed in the staging record.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-126', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A **cardiac patient** with migraine presents for treatment. Which of the following is the most appropriate medication for migraine relief?',
  options:['Ergotamine','IM Ketorolac','Subcutaneous Sumatriptan','IM dihydroergotamine'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> *Ergotamine*: is a vasoconstrictor and can be contraindicated in patients with CVD due to its potential to cause vascular spasm **IM Ketorolac**: is (NSAID) and can be used in the acute treatment of migraine for pain relief. It is generally safe for cardiac patients in appropriate doses **Sumatriptan**: is a serotonin agonist that causes vasoconstriction\n\n**The principle is that three of the four options are vasoconstrictors and the patient has cardiovascular disease.** Ketorolac is the only non-vasoconstrictor on the list, which is what makes it the answer.\n\nExpanding on the box: migraine abortive therapy divides into vasoconstrictor and non-vasoconstrictor arms. The **ergot alkaloids** (ergotamine, dihydroergotamine) and the **triptans** both work partly by constricting dilated cranial vessels — and neither is selective enough to spare the coronary circulation. In a patient with ischaemic heart disease, that constriction can precipitate angina, coronary spasm or infarction. Both classes are therefore **contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension, previous stroke, peripheral vascular disease and Prinzmetal angina** *(the specific contraindication list is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*.\n\n| Option | Class | Vasoconstrictor? | Safe in cardiac disease? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **a. Ergotamine** | Ergot alkaloid | Yes — potent, long-acting, non-selective | **No** — the box calls it contraindicated in CVD |\n| **b. IM Ketorolac** | NSAID | **No** | **Yes** — the key |\n| **c. Subcutaneous sumatriptan** | 5-HT1B/1D agonist | Yes — the box says so explicitly | **No** |\n| **d. IM dihydroergotamine** | Ergot alkaloid | Yes | **No** |\n\nOptions a and d are the *same class*, which is itself a clue: two options that fall together can rarely both be the answer, and here they fall together as ergots. Option c is the drug a student reaches for reflexively, because subcutaneous sumatriptan is the most effective acute migraine treatment available — and effectiveness is not the question. The question is what is **appropriate** for *this* patient.\n\n**Ketorolac** is a parenteral NSAID with strong analgesic potency, widely used for acute migraine in emergency settings. Its own cautions are renal impairment, peptic ulcer disease, bleeding risk and concurrent anticoagulation — none of which the vignette raises, and the box notes it is *"generally safe for cardiac patients in appropriate doses"*. In a cardiac patient also on aspirin, the interaction is worth remembering in practice, though this question does not test it.\n\n**Note on the box\'s own wording:** it explains ergotamine, ketorolac and sumatriptan but says nothing about option d, dihydroergotamine. That is not an oversight of substance — dihydroergotamine is an ergot and falls with option a — but the entry is incomplete as printed.\n\n**Bottom line:** in migraine plus cardiovascular disease, avoid every triptan and every ergot; reach for an NSAID such as ketorolac, or paracetamol with an antiemetic.',
  objective:'Triptans and ergots are vasoconstrictors and are contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease; an NSAID such as IM ketorolac is the appropriate acute migraine treatment in a cardiac patient.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30, explanation box printed). The bank prints option d as "IM dihydromergotamine"; corrected to dihydroergotamine.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-127', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Which of the following **anti-epileptic** drugs is commonly used for preventing migraine?',
  options:['verapamil','Lamotrigine','Topiramate','Carbamazepine'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Topiramate is the antiepileptic with an established place in migraine prophylaxis, and it is the answer.** The question has two filters — the drug must be an antiepileptic *and* a migraine preventive — and only topiramate passes both.\n\n**Topiramate** appears in the supplied material as an antiepileptic: `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` and `L3) MOVEMENT DISORDERS` both carry it. Its mechanisms are multiple — sodium-channel blockade, AMPA/kainate glutamate antagonism, GABA-A potentiation and carbonic anhydrase inhibition — and the same broad action underlies its effect on migraine, where it is one of the two best-evidenced preventives alongside propranolol *(the migraine indication is not taken from the course material, as the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture)*.\n\nIts side-effect profile is heavily examined and clinically decisive: **paraesthesiae** (the carbonic anhydrase effect), **weight loss** — which makes it attractive in obese migraineurs and is unusual among prophylactics — **cognitive slowing and word-finding difficulty**, earning it the nickname "dopamax", **nephrolithiasis**, acute angle-closure glaucoma, and **teratogenicity with a risk of oral clefts**, which matters greatly because migraine prophylaxis is most often prescribed to women of childbearing age.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Is it an antiepileptic? | Is it a migraine preventive? | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **a. verapamil** | **No** — a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker | Yes, but for **cluster headache**, not migraine | Fails the first filter outright, and is the answer to a *different* question in this topic |\n| **b. Lamotrigine** | Yes | **No** — ineffective for migraine headache; it has some reputation for reducing migraine *aura* without reducing the headache | Fails the second filter |\n| **c. Topiramate** | Yes | Yes | **Key** |\n| **d. Carbamazepine** | Yes | **No** — its neurological pain indication is **trigeminal neuralgia** | Fails the second filter |\n\nOption a is the sharpest distractor because verapamil is a real headache prophylactic — the bank itself keys it correct in `npqb-nr-131` for cluster headache — but it is not an antiepileptic. Option d is the second sharpest because carbamazepine is genuinely first-line for a *facial pain*: `L8) Cranial nerves` lists it first among the drugs for trigeminal neuralgia, teaching that treatment uses *"Antiepileptic drugs starting by small dose and gradually increase till the pain is controlled"* — carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, gabapentin, pregabalin. A student who remembers only "antiepileptics are used for head pain" cannot separate d from c; a student who remembers *which* head pain can.\n\n**The other antiepileptic used in migraine prophylaxis is sodium valproate**, which the bank offers as a distractor in `npqb-nr-120`. It is not on this option list, so topiramate is unambiguous here — but if both appeared, both would be defensible, and valproate is avoided in women of childbearing potential because of its far higher teratogenic risk.\n\n**Bottom line:** topiramate for migraine prevention, carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia, verapamil for cluster headache. Three drugs, three head pains, and the examiner will keep swapping them.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Topiramate is the antiepileptic used for migraine prophylaxis; carbamazepine belongs to trigeminal neuralgia and verapamil to cluster headache.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-128', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A female patient with a history of migraine reports improvement with ergotamine during previous attacks. However, she now experiences a **sudden severe** migraine attack that continues without relief until examination. What is the next step in management?',
  options:['CT scan','MRI of the brain','Administer another dose of ergotamine','Intravenous hydration and observe'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> This patient has a sudden severe headache that does not improve with her usual treatment. In this case, a new, severe headache that does not follow the typical pattern of her migraine could suggest a 2ry cause **CT** scan is the first imaging modality to rule out acute intracranial pathology **MRI** is also useful but CT is preferred in an emergency setting to quickly assess for life-threatening conditions like hemorrhage\n\n**The reasoning is the pattern-change red flag applied to a known migraineur.** Having migraine does not protect a patient from subarachnoid haemorrhage — and a headache that is *sudden*, *severe*, and *unlike her usual attacks* is the classic presentation of one.\n\n`L10) Stroke` supplies exactly the contrast the box is relying on: subarachnoid haemorrhage produces *"Thunderclap headache… very severe headache described as the worst headache of my life"* with *"sudden onset of severe headache, in contrast to migrainous hedache, which begins with mild headache then progresses to moderate or severe headache within 30-60 minutes."* This patient\'s attack is described as *sudden* and unresponsive — the migrainous crescendo is absent. The deck also gives the stakes: *"In 85% of the cases, ruptured aneurysm"*, *"A significant proportion of patients die immediately before reaching the hospital"*, and rebleeding that *"can be fatal, so that urgent treatment of the aneurysm is recommended."* The listed investigation is *"CT and/or MRI brain"* followed by CTA/MRA or DSA to demonstrate the aneurysm.\n\n**Why CT and not MRI (option b):** non-contrast CT is fast, universally available out of hours, and is the most sensitive test for **acute blood** — sensitivity for subarachnoid haemorrhage approaches 100% within the first 6 hours and falls thereafter, which is why speed matters. MRI takes far longer, is less accessible in an emergency, is degraded by movement in a patient in severe pain, and is contraindicated with certain implants. MRI is the better test for posterior fossa lesions, tumours and demyelination — none of which is the immediate concern. The box states this directly. *(A negative CT with a persuasive history is followed by lumbar puncture for xanthochromia — not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **c. Administer another dose of ergotamine** — treats the working diagnosis as still correct, which is precisely what the changed pattern forbids. It also risks ergotism with repeated dosing, and would delay diagnosis of a bleed during the window in which rebleeding is most lethal.\n- **d. Intravenous hydration and observe** — a reasonable supportive measure in status migrainosus, and completely wrong as the *next step* here. "Observe" is the one thing that cannot be done with a possible aneurysmal haemorrhage.\n\n**A note on the stem\'s wording:** it calls the event a "migraine attack" while describing features that should stop you accepting that label. That is the whole point of the question — the diagnosis written in the notes is not evidence, and a known migraineur presenting with an atypical headache is being tested for a secondary cause.\n\n**Bottom line:** first or worst headache, or any headache that breaks the established pattern, gets an urgent non-contrast CT.',
  objective:'A known migraineur with a sudden severe headache unlike her usual attacks needs an urgent non-contrast CT to exclude haemorrhage — CT before MRI because it is fastest and most sensitive for acute blood.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-129', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Recurrent unilateral primary headache that lasts for one hour daily at mid night with irritations:',
  options:['Migraine','Cluster headache','Tension type headache','Brain tumor'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Cluster headache — and every clause of this compressed stem is one of its criteria.** *Recurrent* and *primary* place it among the primary headache syndromes; *unilateral* and *one hour* match its strictly one-sided attacks of 15-180 minutes; *daily at mid night* is its clockwork nocturnal periodicity; and *with irritations* refers to the ipsilateral cranial autonomic features — lacrimation, conjunctival injection, rhinorrhoea, ptosis — that accompany the pain.\n\n**⚠️ A transcription note that matters for this entry.** The source misprints the option letters: it prints **a. Migraine, a. Cluster headache, b. Tension type headache, e. Brain tumor** — the letter *a* twice, with *c* and *d* skipped altogether. Verified at 200 dpi, so it is not a scanning artefact. The answer page prints a bare **B** with no name beside it. Read against the *printed* letters, B would resolve to "Tension type headache", which contradicts the stem in every particular; read against the intended sequential lettering, B is the **second option, cluster headache**, which the stem describes exactly. It is held here as the second option. The bank\'s key is preserved — what was defective is the option lettering, not the key.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Fails on |\n|---|---|\n| **Migraine** | Duration is 4-72 hours, not one hour. Associated features are nausea, photophobia and phonophobia, not autonomic "irritations". No clockwork nocturnal timing, and the patient lies still rather than being agitated |\n| **Tension type headache** | **Bilateral**, band-like and pressing, with no autonomic features and no fixed daily timing. It characteristically does *not* wake the patient from sleep, which is the single most useful negative in this stem |\n| **Brain tumor** | Not a **primary** headache — the stem rules it out by definition. A tumour headache is typically progressive over weeks, worse in the morning or on straining, and accompanied by focal signs, papilledema or seizures. `L1) INTRODUCTION AND LOCALISATION IN NEUROLOGY` gives the tempo directly: *"Space occupying lesion (tumor or abscess) - progression over weeks"*, against *"Migraine - progression over seconds to minutes"* |\n\nThe word **primary** is doing real work here, and it is the kind of single-word filter this bank favours. A primary headache is one in which the headache *is* the disease; a secondary headache is a symptom of something else. Naming a brain tumour as a primary headache is a category error, so option d can be eliminated before its clinical features are even considered.\n\n**Cluster headache in brief** *(the syndrome detail is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*: male predominance, onset around the third decade, bouts of weeks to months with long remissions, alcohol triggering attacks during a bout, patients restless and pacing during the pain. Acute treatment is **high-flow 100% oxygen** and **subcutaneous sumatriptan**; prevention is **verapamil**, which the bank keys in `npqb-nr-131`. This same topic asks the diagnosis again in `npqb-nr-123` from a fuller vignette, which is a fair indication of how heavily the examiner weights it.\n\n**Bottom line:** short, strictly unilateral, nocturnal, clockwork, autonomic — cluster headache, and the duration alone separates it from migraine.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Cluster headache is a primary headache of short strictly unilateral attacks with clockwork nocturnal timing and ipsilateral autonomic features; duration separates it from migraine and laterality from tension-type headache.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30). ⚠️ The source misprints the option letters as a, a, b, e — the letter a twice, c and d skipped (verified at 200 dpi). The key is a bare "B" with no name; it is resolved to the second option, cluster headache, which is what the stem describes. The bank prints "that last for one hour"; grammar normalised.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-130', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'What is the mechanism of action of **triptans**?',
  options:['Serotonergic agonists','Calcium channel blockers','Dopamine antagonists','Opioid receptor agonists'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Triptans are serotonin (5-HT) receptor agonists — specifically selective agonists at the 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D subtypes.** The name of the class is a giveaway once known: sumatriptan, zolmitriptan, rizatriptan, naratriptan and eletriptan are all "-triptans", and all are serotonergic.\n\nThe two receptor subtypes account for the two halves of the drug\'s effect, and the two halves of migraine pathophysiology *(this mechanistic account is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture)*:\n\n| Receptor | Location | Effect | What it treats |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **5-HT1B** | Smooth muscle of cranial and meningeal blood vessels | **Vasoconstriction** of the dilated vessels | The vascular component — and the source of the class\'s cardiac contraindications |\n| **5-HT1D** | Presynaptic trigeminal nerve terminals | Inhibits release of **CGRP** and substance P | The neurogenic inflammation component |\n\nA third, central action at brainstem trigeminal nucleus caudalis reduces nociceptive transmission. The net result is an abortive drug that acts on the migraine mechanism itself rather than merely blunting pain perception — which is why triptans work on the nausea and photophobia as well as the headache, and why they work best taken early, before central sensitisation is established.\n\nThe bank confirms this key in its own printed box elsewhere in the topic: the explanation for `npqb-nr-126` states plainly that *"Sumatriptan: is a serotonin agonist that causes vasoconstriction"*. That box is the strongest internal evidence for this answer, and the two questions are on facing pages.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **b. Calcium channel blockers** — a real headache drug class, but a different one and used prophylactically rather than abortively. **Verapamil** is the cluster headache preventive (`npqb-nr-131`) and flunarizine is used for migraine prophylaxis. Nifedipine, offered as a distractor in `npqb-nr-120`, tends to *cause* headache through vasodilatation.\n- **c. Dopamine antagonists** — these do appear in acute migraine care, which makes this a fair distractor: **metoclopramide** and prochlorperazine are given as antiemetics and prokinetics, the former appearing in the bank\'s own keyed answer for `npqb-nr-135` ("Paracetamol plus metoclopramide"). Gastric stasis during a migraine attack impairs absorption of oral analgesics, so the antiemetic serves two purposes. But that is a different drug class from triptans.\n- **d. Opioid receptor agonists** — opioids are actively **discouraged** in migraine. They provide poor relief, cause sedation and nausea, carry dependence risk, and are among the commonest causes of **medication-overuse headache**, converting episodic migraine into a chronic daily headache. This is the opposite of the correct approach and is keyed wrong in `npqb-nr-138` and `npqb-nr-140` as well.\n\n**The clinical consequence of the mechanism:** because 5-HT1B agonism constricts vessels non-selectively, triptans are contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease, uncontrolled hypertension, previous stroke and peripheral vascular disease — the exact reasoning the bank uses to key ketorolac in `npqb-nr-126`. Mechanism and contraindication are the same fact stated twice.\n\n**Bottom line:** triptans are 5-HT1B/1D agonists — vasoconstriction plus inhibition of trigeminal neuropeptide release — and their vasoconstriction is both how they work and why cardiac patients cannot have them.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Triptans are selective 5-HT1B/1D serotonin agonists: 5-HT1B constricts dilated cranial vessels and 5-HT1D inhibits trigeminal CGRP release — which is also why they are contraindicated in cardiovascular disease.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-131', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Which of the following drugs is a **prophylactic** drug for **cluster headache**?',
  options:['Amitriptyline','Verapamil','Sumatriptan','Ibuprofen'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Verapamil is the prophylactic drug of choice for cluster headache.** The question carries two filters again — prophylactic, and *cluster* rather than migraine — and only verapamil satisfies both.\n\n**Verapamil** is a non-dihydropyridine calcium-channel blocker, and its role in cluster headache is one of those facts that must simply be known: it is not predictable from its cardiac indications, and the doses used in cluster headache are typically higher than those used for angina or arrhythmia, which is why **ECG monitoring for heart block and PR prolongation** is required as the dose escalates *(the dosing and monitoring detail is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*. Other side effects are constipation, bradycardia, ankle oedema and gingival hyperplasia.\n\nCluster headache management divides cleanly, and the examiner tests the division:\n\n| | Cluster headache | Migraine |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Abortive** | **High-flow 100% oxygen**; **subcutaneous** sumatriptan | Simple analgesic + antiemetic, NSAIDs, then oral triptan; ergotamine |\n| **Preventive** | **Verapamil** (first line); lithium, topiramate; a tapering steroid course as a short-term bridge | **Propranolol**, **amitriptyline**, sodium valproate, topiramate, flunarizine |\n\nOxygen is worth noting because it is unique to cluster headache and is the one abortive treatment with essentially no systemic toxicity — but it is abortive, and this question asks for prophylaxis.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Amitriptyline** — the strongest distractor, because it *is* a prophylactic. It is simply the wrong headache: amitriptyline prevents **migraine** and **tension-type headache**, and the bank keys it correct for migraine prophylaxis in `npqb-nr-120`. `L8) Cranial nerves` also lists *"Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline"* among the treatments of trigeminal neuralgia. It has no established role in cluster headache. This option punishes the student who has learned "amitriptyline is the headache prophylactic" without learning which headaches.\n- **c. Sumatriptan** — correct disease, wrong purpose. Subcutaneous sumatriptan is the drug of choice to **abort** a cluster attack, and its inclusion is designed to catch a student who recognises it as a cluster drug and stops reading. It cannot be used prophylactically: daily triptan use causes medication-overuse headache, and cluster attacks are far too frequent during a bout for that to be safe.\n- **d. Ibuprofen** — an NSAID with a genuine role in acute migraine and tension-type headache, but no prophylactic role in any of them and essentially no useful effect in cluster headache, whose attacks peak within minutes and are over before an oral NSAID is absorbed. The pharmacokinetics alone rule it out, which is a good illustration of why cluster headache is treated with **subcutaneous** and **inhaled** routes.\n\n**Bottom line:** verapamil prevents cluster headache; oxygen and subcutaneous sumatriptan abort it. Propranolol and amitriptyline prevent migraine. The examiner will keep offering you the right drug for the wrong headache, or the right headache with the wrong purpose.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Verapamil is first-line prophylaxis for cluster headache; oxygen and subcutaneous sumatriptan abort the attack, while amitriptyline and propranolol belong to migraine prophylaxis.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-132', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'What is the most common type of **migraine aura**?',
  options:['Olfactory','Auditory','Gustatory','Visual'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Visual aura is by far the commonest, occurring in around 90% of patients who experience aura at all.** The other three modalities listed are not recognised aura types in migraine, which makes this question a test of one number and one concept.\n\n**What an aura is:** a fully reversible focal neurological disturbance that precedes or accompanies the headache, developing gradually over **5-20 minutes** and lasting **less than 60 minutes**. `L1) INTRODUCTION AND LOCALISATION IN NEUROLOGY` captures the tempo when it lists *"Migraine - progression over seconds to minutes"* among the onset patterns, distinguishing it from vascular events, which are *"Acute or sudden"*, and from a space-occupying lesion, whose *"progression over weeks"* is slower still. That gradual march is the single most useful feature separating an aura from a transient ischaemic attack, which is maximal at onset.\n\nThe underlying mechanism is **cortical spreading depression** — a wave of neuronal depolarisation followed by suppression that moves across the cortex at about 3 mm per minute. It begins in the occipital cortex, which is why the visual modality dominates *(the mechanism is not taken from the course material — no headache deck exists in the supplied lectures)*.\n\n**Forms of visual aura**, in descending frequency: **scintillating scotoma** (a blind spot with a shimmering edge), **fortification spectra or teichopsia** (zigzag lines resembling a fortress wall, expanding across the field), **photopsia** (flashes of light), and less often hemianopic field loss. The bank describes these phenomena in its own vignettes without naming them: `npqb-nr-134` speaks of *"visual disturbances, such as luminous stars or zigzags of light"*, `npqb-nr-135` of *"spots before her eyes"*, and `npqb-nr-119` offers *"colored illusions"* as a distractor. Four questions in one topic lean on visual aura, which is a fair signal of its examinable weight.\n\n**Other genuine aura types**, in order after visual: **sensory** (a unilateral paraesthesia marching from the hand to the face — the cheiro-oral pattern), **speech or language** (transient dysphasia), and **motor** in the rare hemiplegic migraine. `L1) INTRODUCTION AND LOCALISATION IN NEUROLOGY` recognises that last entity by listing *"Migraine syndrome"* among the causes of hemiplegia.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Olfactory** — olfactory hallucinations are not a migraine aura. They are characteristic of **temporal lobe seizures**, where the "uncinate fit" produces an unpleasant smell. `L4) Seizures_Epilepsy` lists *"Foul smells, deja-vu, or rising epigastric sensation"* as the seizure prodrome in its seizure-versus-syncope checklist, and states that *"Aura = focal aware seizure"*. Olfactory phenomena therefore point away from migraine and towards epilepsy — a discrimination worth carrying, since a first seizure and a first migraine aura can both present as a transient neurological event.\n- **b. Auditory** — auditory hallucinations are not a migraine aura either; they belong to psychiatric illness, and phonophobia in migraine is *sensitivity* to sound, not a perception in its absence.\n- **c. Gustatory** — taste phenomena, like olfactory ones, are temporal lobe seizure features rather than migraine aura.\n\n**Bottom line:** migraine aura is visual in about 90% of cases and marches gradually over 5-20 minutes; smell and taste phenomena should make you think of temporal lobe epilepsy instead.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Visual aura — scintillating scotoma and fortification spectra — is the commonest migraine aura at about 90%; olfactory and gustatory phenomena suggest temporal lobe seizures, not migraine.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-133', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A 22-year-old woman presents with daily **band like** headaches for 2 weeks. The headaches are often precipitated by **stress**. Physical examination fails to disclose focal neurologic deficits. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step in patient care?',
  options:['Antidepressant drugs','Calcium-channel antagonists','Ergotamine-containing preparations','NSAIDs'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> The clinical suggest tension type, which are often associated with the neck muscles. Exploration of underlying causes of anxiety is often useful, but a trial with aspirin or other NSAIDs may be sufficient in most cases.\n\n**The diagnosis is tension-type headache and the question asks for the *initial* step — which is simple analgesia, not prophylaxis.** Every clue in the stem points one way: *band-like* is the classic descriptor, *daily* and *bilateral by implication* fit the syndrome, *stress* is its commonest precipitant, and a **normal neurological examination** excludes the secondary causes that would demand imaging instead.\n\n**Tension-type headache** is the commonest primary headache overall — the fact the bank keys separately in `npqb-nr-139`. Its features are a **bilateral**, **pressing or tightening** (non-pulsating) pain of **mild to moderate** intensity, **not aggravated by routine physical activity**, **without nausea or vomiting**, and with at most one of photophobia or phonophobia. Pericranial muscle tenderness is often present on palpation, which is the neck-muscle association the printed box mentions *(the diagnostic criteria are not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture)*.\n\nThe word **initial** is the filter. Two weeks of headache is *episodic*, not chronic — the threshold for chronic tension-type headache is 15 or more headache days per month for over three months. Prophylaxis is not yet indicated. Simple analgesia plus attention to the stressor is the correct first move.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Antidepressant drugs** — this is the sharpest distractor in the topic, because **amitriptyline genuinely is the drug of choice for tension-type headache prophylaxis**, and the bank keys "Antidepressants" correct in `npqb-nr-138`, which asks about **long-term management** of the same condition. The two questions are on facing pages and differ by one word: *initial* against *long-term*. Reading that word is the entire exercise. Prophylaxis is reserved for frequent or chronic tension-type headache, not for a two-week episode.\n- **b. Calcium-channel antagonists** — no established role in tension-type headache. Flunarizine is used in migraine prophylaxis and verapamil in cluster headache; neither applies here.\n- **c. Ergotamine-containing preparations** — an abortive for **migraine**, and inappropriate on three counts: the diagnosis is not migraine; ergots are vasoconstrictors with a poor safety profile; and **daily** use of an ergot in a patient with **daily** headache is a direct route to **medication-overuse headache** and ergotism.\n\n**A caution the box does not raise:** any analgesic taken frequently enough can convert episodic headache into chronic daily headache. NSAIDs are the right initial choice, and the patient should be told to limit them to fewer than about 15 days a month.\n\n**Bottom line:** episodic tension-type headache with a normal examination is treated initially with a simple analgesic or NSAID and management of the stressor; amitriptyline is held back for when it becomes frequent or chronic.',
  objective:'Episodic tension-type headache — bilateral, band-like, stress-precipitated, normal examination — is treated initially with NSAIDs; amitriptyline is for long-term prophylaxis of the frequent or chronic form.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.28 (answer p.30, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-134', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A healthy 20-year-old man presents with a history of recurrent episodes of severe **throbbing** headache. The headaches are triggered or aggravated by **noise and stress** and last for several hours. The pain is frequently preceded by **visual disturbances, such as luminous stars or zigzags of light**, and accompanied by **nausea and vomiting**. His **father experienced a similar** form of headache. The patient has found **no relief with aspirin and ibuprofen**. Which of the following is the most appropriate drug treatment during the **acute** attacks?',
  options:['Acetaminophen','Calcium-channel antagonists','Carbamazepine','Ergotamine','Prednisone'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> The clinical picture is for migraine. Effective drugs include ergotamine tartrate or sumatriptan. CCB for example, have been used for prophylaxis. Acetaminophen is very unlikely to provide relief in classic migraine, especially if aspirin and ibuprofen have already failed to do so\n\n**This is migraine with aura, and because simple analgesics have already failed, the acute treatment must escalate to a specific antimigraine drug — ergotamine is the only one on the list.**\n\nThe vignette is assembled from diagnostic criteria. **Throbbing** (pulsating quality), **lasting several hours** (the 4-72 hour window), **aggravated by noise** (phonophobia), **nausea and vomiting**, and — the clinching feature — *"visual disturbances, such as luminous stars or zigzags of light"*, which is **fortification spectra**, the classic visual aura discussed under `npqb-nr-132`. The **family history** is the final touch: migraine has a strong hereditary component, and the bank keys "hereditary predisposition" as a *true* statement about migraine in `npqb-nr-137`.\n\nThe sentence doing the pharmacological work is *"has found no relief with aspirin and ibuprofen."* That places the patient beyond the first two rungs of the acute migraine ladder — simple analgesic, then NSAID — and at the rung where a specific antimigraine agent is required. The bank spells that ladder out in its own box for `npqb-nr-135`: *"1st line treatment of acute mild to moderate migraine consists of simple analgesic ex paracetamol and an antiemetic. Second line treatment includes the use of NSAIDs. If the above failed, then triptans therapy is indicated."*\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Acetaminophen** | The box addresses this directly: *"very unlikely to provide relief in classic migraine, especially if aspirin and ibuprofen have already failed."* Paracetamol is a weaker analgesic than the NSAIDs that have already failed — prescribing it is a step backwards |\n| **b. Calcium-channel antagonists** | The box again: *"CCB for example, have been used for prophylaxis."* Right disease, wrong purpose — flunarizine prevents migraine, it does not abort an attack |\n| **c. Carbamazepine** | An antiepileptic whose head-pain indication is **trigeminal neuralgia**, taught in `L8) Cranial nerves`: *"Antiepileptic drugs starting by small dose and gradually increase till the pain is controlled. Carbamazepine, Oxcarbazepine, Gabapentin, Pregabalin."* It has no role in migraine, acute or preventive |\n| **e. Prednisone** | A corticosteroid. Its headache role is as a short bridging course in **cluster headache**, and as the emergency treatment of **giant cell arteritis** (`npqb-nr-136`). It is not a migraine abortive |\n\n**Worth noting:** the ideal answer clinically would be a **triptan**, and the box names sumatriptan alongside ergotamine as effective. Sumatriptan is simply not offered. Ergotamine is the older specific antimigraine abortive — a non-selective 5-HT agonist and potent vasoconstrictor, largely displaced by triptans because of a worse side-effect profile, a risk of ergotism, and contraindications in pregnancy and cardiovascular disease. In a healthy 20-year-old man none of those applies, so ergotamine is both the keyed and the defensible answer. **A key is the best option offered, not the best treatment available.**\n\n**Bottom line:** migraine with aura unresponsive to NSAIDs needs a specific abortive — a triptan if available, ergotamine otherwise.',
  objective:'When simple analgesics and NSAIDs fail in acute migraine, escalate to a specific antimigraine abortive — a triptan, or ergotamine where no triptan is offered; calcium-channel blockers are prophylactic and carbamazepine belongs to trigeminal neuralgia.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30, explanation box printed)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-135', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A 17-year-old female presents with **three attacks of headache over a six-month period**. She describes the headaches, right-sided and lasting for **twelve hours** and associated with **nausea and photophobia**. Each is preceded by **spots before her eyes**. What is the most appropriate **initial** treatment for this patient?',
  options:['Diclofenac at the onset of the next attack','Ergotamine suppository at the onset of the next attack','Paracetamol plus metoclopramide at the onset of the next attack','Prophylaxis with propranolol','Sumatriptan at the onset of the next attack'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> 1st line treatment of acute mild to moderate migraine consists of simple analgesic ex paracetamol and an antiemetic. Second line treatment includes the use of NSAIDs. If the above failed, then triptans therapy is indicated. Long-term prophylaxis with drugs (e.g. propranolol) is. only indicated if the attacks are frequent (>2/month)\n\n**This is migraine with aura in a patient who has never been treated, so the answer is the first rung of the ladder: paracetamol plus an antiemetic.** The box lays out both rules the question tests — the acute ladder, and the frequency threshold for prophylaxis.\n\nThe diagnosis is not in doubt: right-sided (unilateral), twelve hours (within the 4-72 hour window), nausea and photophobia, and *"spots before her eyes"* as a visual aura. Compare this vignette with `npqb-nr-134` on the same page, which is also migraine with aura and keys a *different* drug — because that patient had already failed aspirin and ibuprofen and this one has taken nothing. **The two questions differ only in treatment history, and that is what decides both.**\n\n**Why paracetamol *plus metoclopramide*, and not paracetamol alone:** gastric stasis is part of the migraine attack itself, and it impairs absorption of any oral drug given during one. Metoclopramide is a dopamine antagonist that is both an antiemetic and a **prokinetic** — it treats the nausea *and* restores gastric emptying so the analgesic is absorbed. That dual purpose is why the combination, rather than the analgesic alone, is standard first-line therapy *(the absorption rationale is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Diclofenac at the onset** — an NSAID, which the box places at the **second** rung: *"Second line treatment includes the use of NSAIDs."* A reasonable drug, but not the initial step in an untreated patient.\n- **b. Ergotamine suppository at the onset** — a specific antimigraine abortive reserved for attacks that fail simpler measures, and the suppository route acknowledges the vomiting. Far too aggressive as a first step, and ergots carry vasoconstriction risk and a rebound-headache liability.\n- **d. Prophylaxis with propranolol** — the box refutes this arithmetically: prophylaxis *"is only indicated if the attacks are frequent (>2/month)"*. This patient has had **three attacks in six months**, which is 0.5 per month. She does not meet the threshold, and committing a 17-year-old to a daily beta-blocker for an attack rate that low is unjustified. **This option is the trap the whole vignette was built around** — the attack count is stated precisely so it can be tested against the rule.\n- **e. Sumatriptan at the onset** — the box places triptans third: *"If the above failed, then triptans therapy is indicated."* Effective, but not initial.\n\nNote how the option list is constructed: **a, b, c and e are all abortive** and differ only in position on the ladder, while **d is prophylactic**. The question therefore tests two independent decisions at once — abort or prevent, and which rung.\n\n**Bottom line:** untreated mild-to-moderate migraine starts with a simple analgesic plus an antiemetic; escalate to NSAIDs then triptans on failure, and add prophylaxis only when attacks exceed two per month.',
  objective:'First-line acute migraine treatment is a simple analgesic with an antiemetic; NSAIDs are second and triptans third, and prophylaxis is added only when attacks exceed two per month.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30, explanation box printed). The bank prints the stem as "A 1 7-year-old female" with a stray space; read as 17-year-old.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-136', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A **65-year-old** woman presents with a headache that has lasted a few weeks. She gets **pain in her mouth during meals** and her **scalp is tender on palpation**. What is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?',
  options:['Paracetamol','Prednisolone','Amitriptyline','Sumatriptan'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The bank prints an explanation box for this question, transcribed here verbatim:**\n\n> Her symptoms and signs are suggestive of temporal/giant cell arteritis. Treatment of choice is high-dose oral prednisolone\n\n**Giant cell arteritis is an ophthalmic emergency, and high-dose corticosteroid is started immediately — before the biopsy, not after it.** That urgency is the entire point of the question.\n\nThe vignette gives three of the diagnostic pillars. **Age over 50** — giant cell arteritis essentially does not occur below it, and 65 is typical. **Jaw claudication** — *"pain in her mouth during meals"* is the description of it, produced by ischaemia of the masseters supplied by branches of the external carotid, and it is the single most specific symptom of the disease. **Scalp tenderness** — from involvement of the superficial temporal artery, classically noticed when combing the hair or resting the head on a pillow.\n\nThe fuller picture *(the syndrome detail is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck, though `L8) Cranial nerves` does list "giant cell arteritis" among the causes of a sixth-nerve palsy)*: new-onset unilateral temporal headache; a thickened, tender, pulseless temporal artery; constitutional symptoms of fever, malaise and weight loss; an association with **polymyalgia rheumatica** in up to half of patients; and a markedly raised **ESR and CRP**. Diagnosis is confirmed by **temporal artery biopsy**, which shows granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and may miss the lesion through **skip lesions** — so a negative biopsy does not exclude the disease.\n\n**Why treatment cannot wait:** the feared complication is **irreversible blindness** from anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy caused by occlusion of the posterior ciliary arteries. It can occur without warning, is usually permanent once established, and the fellow eye is at risk within days. **Steroid is therefore started on clinical suspicion alone**; the biopsy remains informative for up to about two weeks afterwards, so nothing is lost by treating first. High-dose oral prednisolone is used where vision is intact, and intravenous methylprednisolone where visual loss has already begun.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Paracetamol** — treats the symptom and ignores the disease. Analgesia does nothing to prevent the arteritis from occluding the ophthalmic circulation, and prescribing it here means a patient going blind while comfortable.\n- **c. Amitriptyline** — a prophylactic for migraine and tension-type headache, and listed in `L8) Cranial nerves` for trigeminal neuralgia. It is a preventive for chronic benign headache, and this is neither benign nor chronic.\n- **d. Sumatriptan** — wrong on two counts. The diagnosis is not migraine, and a **vasoconstrictor** given to an elderly woman with an inflammatory arteritis and a compromised ophthalmic circulation is actively dangerous. Triptans are contraindicated in this age group and vascular context in any case.\n\n**The examiner\'s framework:** any **new headache in a patient over 50** is a red flag — the "O" for older age in the SNOOP list — and giant cell arteritis is the diagnosis that framework exists to catch.\n\n**Bottom line:** new headache over 50 with jaw claudication, scalp tenderness and a raised ESR is giant cell arteritis. Start high-dose steroid immediately and arrange the temporal artery biopsy afterwards.',
  objective:'New headache over 50 with jaw claudication and scalp tenderness is giant cell arteritis — start high-dose prednisolone at once to prevent irreversible blindness, and biopsy afterwards.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30, explanation box printed). The bank prints option c as "Amitriptylin"; spelling normalised.'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-137', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Which is **false** regarding migraine?',
  options:['Hereditary predisposition','Always above 50 years','Common in women','Hemicranial headache','Prominent autonomic symptoms'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Migraine characteristically begins in adolescence and early adult life, so "always above 50 years" is the false statement — and the word *always* would give it away even without the epidemiology.**\n\nMigraine typically has its onset between the teens and the thirties, peaks in prevalence in the thirties and forties, and commonly **improves after the menopause**. A **first** migraine over the age of 50 is so unusual that it is itself a red flag — the "O" for older age in the SNOOP list — and should prompt a search for a secondary cause, with **giant cell arteritis** heading the list, as the bank tests in `npqb-nr-136`. The stated option is therefore not merely wrong; it inverts a clinically important rule.\n\n**Why each of the other four is TRUE:**\n\n| Option | Why it is a genuine feature of migraine |\n|---|---|\n| **a. Hereditary predisposition** | Migraine runs strongly in families. The bank uses this itself in `npqb-nr-134`, whose vignette states *"His father experienced a similar form of headache"* as a diagnostic clue. Familial hemiplegic migraine has identified single-gene causes |\n| **c. Common in women** | Migraine is roughly **2-3 times commoner in women** after puberty, and attacks are often menstrually related, reflecting oestrogen withdrawal. Before puberty the sexes are affected about equally, which is itself a favourite examined detail |\n| **d. Hemicranial headache** | *Hemicrania* — "half the head" — is the origin of the word **migraine** itself. Unilateral pain is a core diagnostic criterion, though it may be bilateral in some attacks and in children, as `npqb-nr-120` acknowledges with *"unilateral or bilateral"* |\n| **e. Prominent autonomic symptoms** | Genuinely present in migraine — nausea, vomiting, pallor, sweating and diarrhoea are common, and cranial autonomic features such as lacrimation and nasal congestion occur in a substantial minority of attacks |\n\n**Option e deserves a note**, because it is the one a well-prepared student may hesitate over. Prominent autonomic features are the *hallmark* of the trigeminal autonomic cephalalgias, cluster headache above all — so a student who has just learned that ptosis and lacrimation distinguish cluster from migraine (`npqb-nr-123`, `npqb-nr-129`) may read option e as the false one. The distinction is one of **degree and type**, not of presence: cluster headache has florid, strictly unilateral *cranial* autonomic signs that are a diagnostic requirement, whereas migraine has systemic autonomic symptoms and milder, inconstant cranial ones. Since the question asks which statement is **false**, and option b is flatly and dangerously false, option e is not the answer. *(This comparison is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture.)*\n\n**A word on technique:** absolute qualifiers — *always*, *never*, *only* — are rarely true in clinical medicine, and in a "which is false" question they are the first thing to examine. Here that instinct and the epidemiology agree.\n\n**Bottom line:** migraine starts young, favours women, is hemicranial, runs in families and carries autonomic symptoms. A **first** headache over 50 is not migraine until giant cell arteritis and a structural lesion have been excluded.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Migraine begins in adolescence or early adulthood and is 2-3 times commoner in women; a first headache over 50 is a red flag for a secondary cause, not migraine.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-138', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Which of the following medications is most commonly used for **long-term management** of **Tension-type headache**?',
  options:['Antidepressants','Opioids','Corticosteroids','Triptans'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Antidepressants — specifically the tricyclic amitriptyline — are the mainstay of long-term prophylaxis in tension-type headache.** The phrase *long-term management* is the filter, and it is the deliberate counterpart of `npqb-nr-133` on the facing page, which asks for the **initial** step in the same condition and keys **NSAIDs**. The bank is testing one distinction twice.\n\n| | Episodic / initial | Frequent or chronic / long-term |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tension-type headache** | Simple analgesic or **NSAID** at the time of the headache (`npqb-nr-133`) | **Amitriptyline** taken daily (this question) |\n\n**Amitriptyline** is a tricyclic antidepressant, and its analgesic effect is **independent of any antidepressant action** — it works at doses well below the antidepressant range, begins to help within weeks rather than the months an antidepressant response takes, and helps patients who are not depressed at all. The mechanism is inhibition of serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake, augmenting descending pain-modulating pathways, with additional sodium-channel and anticholinergic effects. This point is worth stating to patients explicitly, because being handed an "antidepressant" for a physical pain is commonly misread as being told the pain is imaginary.\n\nThe supplied material corroborates the class in a neighbouring condition: `L8) Cranial nerves` lists among the treatments of trigeminal neuralgia *"Antidepressants: Tricyclic antidepressants: amitriptyline – Selective serotonin-noradrenaline reuptake inhibitors; duloxetine"*. The same drugs used at the same low doses for the same reason. *(The specific tension-type headache indication is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck.)*\n\nSide effects are largely anticholinergic and dose-limiting: dry mouth, sedation — usefully exploited by dosing at night — constipation, urinary retention, blurred vision, weight gain and postural hypotension. In overdose tricyclics are cardiotoxic, which matters in a population with comorbid depression.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **b. Opioids** — contraindicated in the long-term management of *any* primary headache. They give poor relief, cause tolerance and dependence, and are among the commonest causes of **medication-overuse headache**, converting an episodic problem into a chronic daily one. The bank keys opioids wrong again in `npqb-nr-140`.\n- **c. Corticosteroids** — no role in tension-type headache. Their headache uses are a short bridging course in cluster headache and the emergency treatment of **giant cell arteritis** (`npqb-nr-136`). Long-term steroids would bring osteoporosis, diabetes, hypertension and immunosuppression for no benefit.\n- **d. Triptans** — abortive drugs for **migraine** and cluster headache, ineffective in tension-type headache because it does not involve the trigeminovascular activation triptans act on. They are abortive, not prophylactic, and daily use causes medication-overuse headache.\n\n**A practical point the option list omits:** long-term management of tension-type headache is not only pharmacological. Stress management, sleep hygiene, correction of posture and workstation ergonomics, physiotherapy for pericranial muscle tenderness, and cognitive behavioural therapy all have a place, and limiting acute analgesic use to under about 15 days a month is essential to prevent medication overuse.\n\n**Bottom line:** amitriptyline for frequent or chronic tension-type headache; NSAIDs for the individual episode; never opioids.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Amitriptyline is the long-term prophylactic for frequent or chronic tension-type headache and works at sub-antidepressant doses; NSAIDs treat the individual episode and opioids are never appropriate.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-139', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most common type of primary headache**?',
  options:['Migraine','Cluster headache','Tension-type headache','Trigeminal neuralgia'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Tension-type headache is the commonest primary headache, and by a wide margin — lifetime prevalence around 40-70%, against roughly 12-15% for migraine.** The ranking is the whole question.\n\nThe order to memorise is: **tension-type >> migraine >> cluster**. It is worth being clear about *why* the ranking is so often got wrong. Tension-type headache is the commonest headache **experienced**; migraine is the commonest headache **presenting to a doctor**, because it is disabling enough to make people seek help, while most tension-type headache is self-treated and never reaches a clinic. A student who has learned "migraine is the commonest headache we see" has learned something true about clinical practice and false about epidemiology. This question asks about prevalence.\n\n| | Lifetime prevalence | Character | Relative frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tension-type headache** | ~40-70% | Bilateral, band-like, pressing, mild-moderate, no nausea | **Commonest** |\n| **Migraine** | ~12-15% | Unilateral, pulsating, moderate-severe, nausea/photophobia, ± aura | Second — and the commonest reason to *consult* |\n| **Cluster headache** | ~0.1% | Strictly unilateral, boring, periorbital, 15-180 min, autonomic features | Rare |\n\n*(These prevalence figures are not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series contains no headache lecture.)*\n\n**The word *primary* is the second filter, and it is what eliminates option d.** A **primary** headache is one in which the headache is the disease itself; a **secondary** headache is a symptom of some other disorder — tumour, haemorrhage, meningitis, giant cell arteritis, raised intracranial pressure.\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Migraine** — a genuine primary headache and the strongest distractor, but second in prevalence for the reason set out above.\n- **b. Cluster headache** — a primary headache, and among the *rarest* at roughly 0.1% prevalence with a marked male predominance. It is heavily examined because it is dramatic and has a distinctive treatment, which can leave students overestimating how common it is.\n- **d. Trigeminal neuralgia** — **not a headache at all in this classification.** It is a cranial **neuralgia**, a facial pain, and it is uncommon. `L8) Cranial nerves` describes it as *"one of the most common causes of facial pain"* — note *facial pain*, not headache — and adds that *"TN is one of the most frequently seen neuralgias in the elderly"* and *"usually starts between the ages of 50 and 60."* The deck also notes it is *"divided by etiology into classic and secondary categories by the International Headache Society (IHS) criteria"*, in which secondary trigeminal neuralgia is caused by *"structural lesions other than vascular compression e.g., multiple sclerosis, post-herpetic, and tumor."* So trigeminal neuralgia can itself be secondary, doubly disqualifying it here.\n\n**Note on the same fact elsewhere in the corpus:** this fact is printed in the ENT Grade Gain book too, with one distractor changed — a genuinely separate question rather than a reprint, and both are held.\n\n**Bottom line:** tension-type headache is the commonest primary headache; migraine is the commonest one that reaches a doctor; cluster headache is rare; trigeminal neuralgia is a facial neuralgia, not a primary headache.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Tension-type headache is the commonest primary headache (~40-70% lifetime), ahead of migraine (~12-15%) and cluster headache (~0.1%); trigeminal neuralgia is a cranial neuralgia, not a primary headache.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30)'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-140', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-headache',
  stem:'A 30-year-old male presents with a dull, aching headache that has been bothering him for the past three days. The pain is **bilateral**, and feels like a **"band" around his head**. The patient reports **muscle stiffness in the neck and shoulders**. What is the **1st line approach**?',
  options:['Triptans','Lifestyle modifications','Opioid analgesics','Corticosteroids'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**This is tension-type headache, and the bank keys lifestyle modification as the first-line approach.** The vignette is diagnostic: **bilateral**, **dull and aching** rather than pulsating, described as a **band** around the head, and accompanied by **pericranial muscle stiffness in the neck and shoulders**, which is the muscle involvement the bank\'s own box for `npqb-nr-133` calls *"often associated with the neck muscles."*\n\nThe reasoning behind the key is that tension-type headache is strongly driven by modifiable factors, and that in a three-day episode in an otherwise well 30-year-old the correct first move is to address the cause rather than to escalate drugs. The lifestyle measures with an evidence base *(this list is not taken from the course material — the supplied neurology series has no headache deck)*: **stress management and relaxation training**, **regular sleep**, **posture and workstation ergonomics** — decisive when neck and shoulder stiffness is present, as here — **regular exercise**, adequate **hydration**, limiting caffeine, and **physiotherapy or massage** for pericranial muscle tenderness. Cognitive behavioural therapy and biofeedback are effective in frequent cases. Crucially, lifestyle measures carry **no risk of medication-overuse headache**, which is the principal hazard of treating a recurring headache with drugs.\n\n**⚠️ A note on this key, recorded rather than disputed.** The bank keys **NSAIDs** as the appropriate initial step for tension-type headache in `npqb-nr-133`, and its printed box there states that *"a trial with aspirin or other NSAIDs may be sufficient in most cases."* Here it keys **lifestyle modifications** for the same condition. The two are not strictly contradictory — the option lists differ, and **NSAIDs are not offered here at all**, so lifestyle modification is the best of what is available and no other option is defensible. But a learner meeting both questions should know that the bank\'s answer for "first-line tension-type headache" depends on the menu, and that in practice the two are complementary: simple analgesia for the episode, lifestyle change to reduce recurrence. **Read the option list before answering.**\n\n**Why the others are wrong:**\n\n- **a. Triptans** — abortive drugs for **migraine** and cluster headache. They act on trigeminovascular activation, which is not the mechanism of tension-type headache, and they are simply ineffective in it. They also carry vasoconstrictor contraindications and a medication-overuse liability.\n- **c. Opioid analgesics** — wrong for any primary headache, and particularly wrong as a *first-line* approach in a 30-year-old with a three-day benign headache. Poor efficacy, sedation, tolerance, dependence, and a leading cause of **medication-overuse headache**. The bank keys opioids wrong in `npqb-nr-138` as well.\n- **d. Corticosteroids** — no role in tension-type headache. Their headache indications are a bridging course in cluster headache and the emergency treatment of **giant cell arteritis** (`npqb-nr-136`), a disease of the over-50s that this 30-year-old with a normal examination does not have.\n\n**When to escalate:** if the headaches become frequent (over about 15 days a month) or chronic, add **amitriptyline** as prophylaxis, which is what `npqb-nr-138` keys for long-term management. Investigate only if red flags appear — a focal neurological sign, a changed pattern, a thunderclap onset, or first onset over 50.\n\n**Bottom line:** tension-type headache is managed first by removing what drives it — stress, posture, sleep — with simple analgesia for the episode and amitriptyline reserved for the frequent or chronic form. Never opioids.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Tension-type headache is managed first with lifestyle modification — stress, sleep, posture and ergonomics — with simple analgesia for the episode; triptans, opioids and steroids have no place.',
  source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.29 (answer p.30). ⚠️ The bank keys NSAIDs for the same condition in npqb-nr-133 where NSAIDs are on the menu; here they are not offered. Noted, not disputed.'
}
